<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PermissionSet xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        This comment sits INSIDE the root element deliberately. A comment placed ABOVE
        <PermissionSet> breaks `sf` at source conversion with the misleading error "unable to
        find matching parent xml file". Broker_Protection_Access and DPEG_Admin_Access are
        precedents for this pattern (Disposition_Deal_Driver was too, until it was retired
        2026-08-19 - see section 1 below); it is used here because the rationale below is far
        longer than the 255-character cap on <description>.

        WHAT THIS SET IS (added 2026-08-10). It is the Opportunity twin of
        Lead_Stage_Actions_Access: a LAYER-4 CAPABILITY set, carrying Apex invoke access for the
        stage quick actions and nothing else. Four classAccesses is the whole file. Every
        omission below is deliberate and each is argued in its own paragraph - none of them is
        an oversight, and none of them should be "completed" by a later tidy-up pass.

        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        1. WHY THERE IS NO Acquisition_Deal_Actions GRANT HERE - THE TWO-FACTOR SEPARATION
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        THE RULE: this set carries the CAPABILITY (invoke the controllers).
        Acquisition_Deal_Driver carries the AUTHORIZATION. They are two factors of one gate
        and must never be merged, in either direction. DO NOT ADD A customPermissions ENTRY
        FOR Acquisition_Deal_Actions (OR Disposition_Deal_Actions) TO THIS FILE, and do not
        add a fieldPermissions entry for any User.* field either.

        🔬 UPDATED 2026-08-12 — the AUTHORIZATION mechanism changed; the SEPARATION did not.
        Through PHASE 1 of the driver-flag retirement (see
        agent-output/design-requirements-custom-permissions.md), then a later developer pass,
        OpportunityActionPermissionService.hasDealActionAccess() was moved off
        User.Deal_Driver__c FLS onto FeatureManagement.checkPermission('Acquisition_Deal_
        Actions'). It still asks two independent questions (ARCHITECTURE.md section 2,
        "Opportunity deal-action gate is TWO-FACTOR"):
          (a) CAPABILITY    - can the user reach the code at all? Today: Apex class access,
                              granted by THIS set.
          (b) AUTHORIZATION - does the user hold the Acquisition_Deal_Actions custom
                              permission? Granted by Acquisition_Deal_Driver ONLY.

        Acquisition_Deal_Driver exists solely to carry (b). If this set also granted that
        custom permission, the gate would not break today - the population is identical to
        this set's own - but the SEPARATION would be gone, and the next reviewer who finds an
        authorization grant sitting in a capability set will reasonably fold the two together,
        at which point MEMBERSHIP SILENTLY BECOMES AUTHORIZATION for this set's whole
        population, exactly as the retired FLS version of this rule warned.
        OpportunityActionPermissionServiceTest.capabilitySetWithoutTheAuthorizationSet_isStillDenied
        (renamed from ...hasDealActionAccess_membershipWithoutTheFlag_isStillDenied when the
        mechanism moved) is the falsifier that goes red if anyone tries; do not modify that
        test. User.Deal_Driver__c and its fieldPermissions grant on Acquisition_Deal_Driver
        are RETIRED - they govern nothing and are kept only as a rollback lever pending
        deletion in a separate, later phase.

        The identical rule governed Disposition_Deal_Actions / Disposition_Deal_Driver, which
        RecordStageAdvanceController reaches through the Disposition_NDA record type's gate.
        Neither authorization grant belongs in this file - that has not changed.

        🔴 RETRACTED IN PART, 2026-08-19, EXPLICIT USER DECISION - DISPOSITION ONLY, NOT
        ACQUISITION. Disposition_Deal_Driver, the dedicated authorization set named above, was
        retired. Its payload (the Disposition_Deal_Actions custom permission) did not go with
        it: it now rides permissionsets/DPEG_Disposition_Edit.permissionset-meta.xml, a MODULE
        DATA set, not a dedicated authorization set. Disposition is now an explicit, user-chosen
        EXCEPTION to the two-factor separation this section argues for - see that file's own
        header, and permissionsetgroups/DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG.permissionsetgroup-meta.xml,
        for the full rationale and consequence (every DPEG_Disposition_Edit holder, and so the
        whole DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG population, is now a disposition deal driver with no
        separate assignment step).

        THE REASONING IN THIS SECTION IS UNCHANGED AND STILL GOVERNS ACQUISITION. Nothing above
        this note was altered: Acquisition_Deal_Driver still carries Acquisition_Deal_Actions as
        a separate, directly-assigned layer-5 set, and that separation must not be weakened. Do
        not read the disposition exception as license to fold Acquisition_Deal_Actions into
        DPEG_Acquisition_Edit or into this capability set to "match" the disposition side - the
        two modules are now deliberately asymmetric, not inconsistent, and DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG's
        own header warns against "fixing" that asymmetry in either direction without a further
        decision.

        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        2. WHY THERE ARE NO objectPermissions - AND WHY THIS DIFFERS FROM THE LEAD TWIN
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        Lead_Stage_Actions_Access carries Account / Contact / Lead object grants because
        Database.convertLead writes to Account and Contact, which the Lead module sets do not
        otherwise cover. The Opportunity actions have no analogue: DPEG_Acquisition_Edit already
        grants Opportunity and all six stage-controlled child objects (LOI__c, NDA__c,
        Underwriting__c, Contract_Review__c, Development_Feasibility_Review__c,
        Construction_Feasibility_Review__c), and every deal driver holds it through
        DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG. Adding those objects here would create exactly the duplication
        this set was introduced alongside a cleanup to REMOVE. Omitting them is the point.

        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        3. WHY THERE ARE NO fieldPermissions AT ALL - MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        RecordStageAdvanceService.load() dispatches to each object's own selectStageRequiredById,
        all of which are WITH USER_MODE - and USER_MODE THROWS rather than degrades, so an
        ungranted field would be a hard failure, not a blank. Every stage field those six queries
        select was checked against DPEG_Acquisition_Edit UNION DPEG_Disposition_Edit on
        2026-08-10, in BOTH the deployed org state (retrieved from usman-dpeg) and the repo
        state, and all six are readable AND editable in both:

            NDA__c.Status__c                             LOI__c.Stage__c
            Underwriting__c.Stage__c                     Contract_Review__c.Negotiation_Status__c
            Construction_Feasibility_Review__c.Stage__c  Development_Feasibility_Review__c.Stage__c

        The seventh selected field, RecordTypeId, carries no field-level security at all, so it
        cannot be granted anywhere and needs no entry. Those FLS grants are load-bearing, but
        they live in the MODULE DATA sets where they belong. Duplicating them here would put one
        grant in two homes and would make this file a second place to maintain when a stage field
        is renamed.

        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        4. WHY THERE ARE NO userPermissions
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        Lead_Stage_Actions_Access carries ConvertLeads because Lead conversion is gated by a
        platform user permission. Opportunity stage advance and Submit for Approval have no
        equivalent platform user permission. There is nothing to grant, not something omitted.

        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        5. TWO OF THESE FOUR CLASS GRANTS ARE A DELIBERATE DUPLICATE OF DPEG_Apex_Access
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        THIS IS THE SPECIFIC THING A FUTURE "NO DUPLICATION" PASS WILL TRY TO REMOVE. It is
        correct and it must stay. OpportunityActionPermissionController and
        RecordStageAdvanceController are granted BOTH here and in DPEG_Apex_Access, because both
        hold a permission-QUESTION method that a NOT-authorized user must still be able to
        reach so it can honestly answer false. If class access were narrowed to the deal-driver
        population, an unauthorized user's call would fail with an Apex access error instead of a
        clean denial, and lwc/dealActionGuard and lwc/recordStageGuard both surface
        error.body.message straight into a toast - so the user would see raw platform text
        ("You do not have access to the Apex class named ..."), which ARCHITECTURE.md section 5
        forbids. The gate still fails CLOSED, so this is a message-quality and diagnosability
        defect rather than a security hole - but it is an avoidable regression.

        RecordStageAdvanceController is worse than a preference: APEX CLASS ACCESS IS PER-CLASS,
        NOT PER-METHOD, and that one class holds both the cacheable question
        (hasStageActionAccess, called by recordStageGuard for anyone who clicks) and the actions
        (advance / advanceTo). It is structurally un-narrowable.

        Lead_Stage_Actions_Access already does exactly this with LeadActionPermissionController,
        for exactly this reason. That is the established precedent, not an inconsistency.

        Only the two ACTION-ONLY classes were shed from DPEG_Apex_Access in the same change -
        StageAdvanceController and OpportunityApprovalController - because both are reachable
        only after a guard has already returned true, so a non-driver never calls them.

        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        6. THE NAME SAYS "OPPORTUNITY"; THE SET ALSO CARRIES A DISPOSITION PATH (Gate 1, Q1)
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        RecordStageAdvanceController also serves the Disposition_NDA record type, whose gate is
        DISPOSITION_DRIVER / User.Disposition_Driver__c - NOT the Opportunity gate. So a set
        named for Opportunity carries the capability for a disposition action too.

        This is harmless today only because DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG is the single persona holding
        both DPEG_Acquisition_Edit and DPEG_Disposition_Edit. IT BECOMES A NAMING DEFECT THE DAY A
        DISPOSITION-ONLY PERSONA EXISTS, who would then be assigned an "Opportunity" set in order
        to drive a sale. The name was accepted at Gate 1 as requested; splitting into two
        capability sets now would add a set to solve a problem nobody has. Revisit at the moment a
        disposition-only persona is created - that is the trigger, not a calendar date.

        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        7. SEQUENCING - THIS SET AND THE DPEG_Apex_Access TRIM ARE ONE CHANGE, IN ORDER
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        While the four classes remain in DPEG_Apex_Access (which is in all four PSGs), adding
        this set to DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG grants ZERO additional effective access - permission
        sets union, so the classes are already reachable. This set only becomes load-bearing at
        the moment the two action controllers leave the catch-all. Therefore: DEPLOY AND JOIN
        FIRST, WAIT FOR PERMISSION SET GROUP RECALCULATION, VERIFY, TRIM SECOND. Never the
        reverse - the reverse order breaks the live deal-driver buttons between the two deploys.

        ASSIGNMENT IS AN ORG ACTION, NOT DEPLOYABLE METADATA. Deploying this set grants nobody
        anything until DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG finishes recalculating.
        🔬 UPDATED 2026-08-12 — the retired ordering rule below no longer applies. It used to
        read: "an administrator smoke test proves nothing about this gate: a bare System
        Administrator has no FLS on User.Deal_Driver__c either (Metadata-API-deployed custom
        fields arrive with no field permissions for ANY profile), which is why the service
        checks Modify All Data BEFORE it reads the flag." FeatureManagement.checkPermission
        has no FLS-provisioning gap for an administrator to be locked out by, so
        OpportunityActionPermissionService now checks the Modify All Data bypass AFTER the
        custom-permission read - the code has deliberately INVERTED the order this note used
        to require. An administrator smoke test still proves nothing about a NON-admin's
        access, because Modify All Data bypasses the check entirely either way - acceptance-
        test as a real deal-driver persona.
    -->
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>OpportunityActionPermissionController</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>OpportunityApprovalController</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>RecordStageAdvanceController</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>StageAdvanceController</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <description>Capability set for the Opportunity and child-object stage quick actions: Apex invoke access only. It does NOT grant Acquisition_Deal_Actions - that is Acquisition_Deal_Driver&apos;s job and the gate is two-factor. Assign with DPEG_Acquisition_Edit.</description>
    <hasActivationRequired>false</hasActivationRequired>
    <label>Opportunity Stage Actions Access</label>
</PermissionSet>
